;;; The eight `y` facts alone derive `(false)` — the control of M1e2 **W23**,
;;; register row `W23/core-search-cut` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked
;;; by S1e2.2.23 (2026-11-05) from the inventory of 2026-11-05's F2
;;; (`plans/m1e2_correctness/inventory_2026-11-05/findings/F2_core_cut_control.ein`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `core-search-cut.ein` with its eight `x` facts deleted, and nothing else.
;;; `(A k)` now has one environment at root, the eight `y` facts, and
;;; `(B k)` the same, so root is dead and its core is those eight facts. The
;;; `x` relations are declared and no fact names them, so the blind
;;; enumerator proposes `(x1 k)` … `(x8 k)`: eight candidates, each dead
;;; alone, and a cube of 256 nodes.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R is empty, every node dead. Root's core is `(y1 k) … (y8 k)`, the
;;; smallest set of givens that derives `(false)`, and its search is inside
;;; its budget. A node that commits `x` facts gives `(A k)` an environment
;;; per choice of `xi` or `yi` over the committed `i`: 2^c of them. Past 64,
;;; at seven or eight `x` facts, the search is cut, as the witness's is, and
;;; reports a core of nine or ten facts where `(y1 k) … (y8 k)` is still the
;;; smallest: 9 of the cube's 256 nodes, which `ein oracle --dump` writes.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what holds since ─────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `e2a3263e`, release and debug: R empty, exit 0, root's core the eight
;;; `y` facts. Until S1e2.2.23, `ein oracle --dump`'s `region.json` gave the
;;; nine cut nodes cores of nine and ten facts with nothing to say they were
;;; cut; since its fix each says `core_status: "cut-environments"`, and the
;;; drawn table *core search cut at its budget of 64 environments per fact*,
;;; and root's says `within-budget`, in either build.

(relation x1 T) (relation y1 T) (relation p1 T) (relation x2 T) (relation y2 T) (relation p2 T) (relation x3 T) (relation y3 T) (relation p3 T) (relation x4 T) (relation y4 T) (relation p4 T) (relation x5 T) (relation y5 T) (relation p5 T) (relation x6 T) (relation y6 T) (relation p6 T) (relation x7 T) (relation y7 T) (relation p7 T) (relation x8 T) (relation y8 T) (relation p8 T) (relation A T) (relation B T)
(y1 k) (y2 k) (y3 k) (y4 k) (y5 k) (y6 k) (y7 k) (y8 k)
(rule lift (?X ?P) :match (?X ?v) :assert (?P ?v))
(lift x1 p1) (lift y1 p1) (lift x2 p2) (lift y2 p2) (lift x3 p3) (lift y3 p3) (lift x4 p4) (lift y4 p4) (lift x5 p5) (lift y5 p5) (lift x6 p6) (lift y6 p6) (lift x7 p7) (lift y7 p7) (lift x8 p8) (lift y8 p8)
(rule mk-a () :match (and (p1 ?v) (p2 ?v) (p3 ?v) (p4 ?v) (p5 ?v) (p6 ?v) (p7 ?v) (p8 ?v)) :assert (A ?v))
(rule mk-b () :match (and (y1 ?v) (y2 ?v) (y3 ?v) (y4 ?v) (y5 ?v) (y6 ?v) (y7 ?v) (y8 ?v)) :assert (B ?v))
(rule clash () :match (and (A ?v) (B ?v)) :assert (false))
